    "note_tweet": {
      "entities": {},
      "text": "China is merely an Asian country, essentially an ancient battlefield in the Central Plains, and its primary focus is not tourism.\n\nUberChina was acquired by Didi Chuxing a long time ago, and it can even be linked to Visa or MasterCard cards, just like Grab in Southeast Asia.\n\nIn most first-tier cities, subway systems now support credit card payments via swipe, eliminating the need to purchase tickets. Credit cards can also be used directly at merchants that provide POS machines (usually UnionPay Merchant Services) such as Starbucks and Haidilao.\nIf you have a UnionPay credit card, you can download the \"UnionPay\"（云闪付） or \"Hello China\"（你好中国） App, and then bind it to your account for QR code payments or have it scanned by merchants.\nIf you only have Visa, MasterCard, or American Express, you can only link Alipay or WeChat Pay, because most of us are small businesses and are unwilling to pay transaction fees.\n\nJust like Japanese people speak Japanese, why do Chinese people have to speak English? Besides, can't you just buy a SIM card? You can use the \"Hello China\" or RedteaGO ($3 promo code SUJI0003) mentioned above to get an eSIM service without a VPN. Are you kidding me?🤣\n\nChina has only recently begun its visa-free transit policy, and previously, obtaining a Chinese visa was not much more difficult than obtaining a US visa.\nI don't think it's necessary to engage in pointless criticism based on differences in systems. As Premier Zhou Enlai said, \"Seek common ground while reserving differences,\" mutual understanding and world peace are essential for a better world."
    },
